1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,539
I need a bracket that holds a stepper motor on an aluminium extrusion.

2
00:00:04,539 --> 00:00:07,460
Bent from sheet metal, and cut by a laser service.

3
00:00:08,160 --> 00:00:11,774
Today I'll show you how to design it without any CAD knowledge.

4
00:00:11,774 --> 00:00:17,970
An AI agent builds the bracket around the real motor and extrusion, and unfolds it into a DXF for the laser.

5
00:00:20,651 --> 00:00:21,409
We use SOMA.

6
00:00:21,409 --> 00:00:25,581
It's free and open source, and you can download it at somacad.app.

7
00:00:27,231 --> 00:00:30,281
I start a new model and import two STEP files:

8
00:00:30,281 --> 00:00:34,789
a NEMA 17 stepper motor and a 100 mm piece of 2020 V-slot extrusion.

9
00:00:34,789 --> 00:00:39,761
Both come from the FreeCAD parts library, under a Creative Commons license.

10
00:00:40,698 --> 00:00:44,017
Click a STEP file, and SOMA shows it in the viewer.

11
00:00:44,017 --> 00:00:45,514
This is the real motor:

12
00:00:45,514 --> 00:00:48,508
flange, shaft and connector, ten parts in all.

13
00:00:49,208 --> 00:00:52,458
The files sit in the inputs folder, where the agent can use them.

14
00:00:54,108 --> 00:00:56,621
Skills tell the agent how your parts are made.

15
00:00:56,621 --> 00:01:00,718
There's one for sheet metal, and also for laser cutting and plasma cutting.

16
00:01:01,418 --> 00:01:04,091
I set the sheet metal skill to my material:

17
00:01:04,091 --> 00:01:06,702
2 mm steel, with a 2 mm inner bend radius.

18
00:01:06,702 --> 00:01:08,753
The agent then follows its rules:

19
00:01:08,753 --> 00:01:14,348
distances from holes to bends, bend reliefs, and the flat pattern with the right K-factor.

20
00:01:16,408 --> 00:01:17,301
Now the agent.

21
00:01:17,301 --> 00:01:20,938
Click the plus above the terminal, and start Claude Code.

22
00:01:23,375 --> 00:01:25,709
I describe what I need in plain words:

23
00:01:25,709 --> 00:01:33,265
the motor at the end of the extrusion, the shaft sideways for a belt, slots to tension the belt, and M5 screws with T-nuts.

24
00:01:34,583 --> 00:01:40,220
The agent measures the motor and the extrusion in the STEP files, picks the screws, and builds the bracket.

25
00:01:40,220 --> 00:01:43,433
Every time it changes the model, SOMA builds it and shows it.

26
00:01:44,376 --> 00:01:44,976
Here it is.

27
00:01:44,976 --> 00:01:50,426
The structure tree lists the bracket and the screws, with the motor and the extrusion as references.

28
00:01:51,743 --> 00:01:53,176
It's a C-shaped channel.

29
00:01:53,176 --> 00:01:54,729
The plate holds the motor:

30
00:01:54,729 --> 00:01:59,089
the pilot hole, and the 31 mm hole pattern as slots for the belt tension.

31
00:01:59,089 --> 00:02:02,433
Two flanges wrap around the extrusion and make it stiff.

32
00:02:03,183 --> 00:02:06,593
Explode pulls the parts apart, so you can see how everything goes together.

33
00:02:08,216 --> 00:02:09,895
A section cuts through the middle:

34
00:02:09,895 --> 00:02:13,946
the two bends with their radius, and the screws going into the T-nuts in the slot.

35
00:02:15,596 --> 00:02:17,706
Now the file for the laser service.

36
00:02:17,706 --> 00:02:24,278
I ask for the flat pattern as a DXF, with the bend lines on their own layer, and a drawing with a bend table.

37
00:02:24,978 --> 00:02:26,617
The agent unfolds the bracket.

38
00:02:26,617 --> 00:02:31,588
Each bend adds a little length, worked out from the radius, the thickness and the K-factor.

39
00:02:32,538 --> 00:02:35,197
Click the DXF, and SOMA opens it right here:

40
00:02:35,197 --> 00:02:39,306
the outline and holes to cut, and the bend lines on their own layer.

41
00:02:40,006 --> 00:02:40,954
And the drawing:

42
00:02:40,954 --> 00:02:46,344
the flat blank with its size, and a table with every bend, its direction, angle and radius.

43
00:02:47,994 --> 00:02:48,908
One more thing:

44
00:02:48,908 --> 00:02:50,798
a small tab for a limit switch.

45
00:02:50,798 --> 00:02:55,004
With Mark up, I simply draw it where I want it, and write what it is.

46
00:02:56,494 --> 00:02:58,784
Send it to the agent, and add one sentence.

47
00:03:02,969 --> 00:03:05,931
There's the tab, with its own bend and holes for the switch.

48
00:03:05,931 --> 00:03:07,659
And the flat pattern grows with it.

49
00:03:08,409 --> 00:03:11,397
The agent also made the main sizes adjustable.

50
00:03:11,397 --> 00:03:15,099
In the Parameters tab, I change the sheet from 2 to 3 mm.

51
00:03:15,799 --> 00:03:18,890
The rebuild takes a moment, the motor model is quite detailed.

52
00:03:18,890 --> 00:03:22,729
Then the bracket is thicker, and the flat pattern has the new bend allowance.

53
00:03:23,429 --> 00:03:26,001
Open the DXF again, and it's up to date.

54
00:03:26,001 --> 00:03:27,159
Ready to send off.

55
00:03:27,876 --> 00:03:31,934
Upload it to a laser cutting service, bend the flanges, and bolt the motor on.

56
00:03:31,934 --> 00:03:35,992
And if you cut your own parts on a plasma table, there's a skill for that too.

57
00:03:37,442 --> 00:03:42,458
From two STEP files to a flat pattern for the laser, without drawing a single line.

58
00:03:42,458 --> 00:03:44,452
Get SOMA for free at somacad.app.
